  author    = "Mulhern, Christine",
  journal   = "American Economic Review",
  publisher = "American Economic Association",
  volume    =  113,
  number    =  11,
  pages     = "2846--2893",
  abstract  = "Counselors are a common school resource for students navigating
               complicated and consequential education choices. I estimate
               counselors’ causal effects using quasi-random assignment policies
               in Massachusetts. Counselors vary substantially in their
               effectiveness at increasing high school graduation and college
               attendance, selectivity, and persistence. Counselor effects on
               educational attainment are similar in magnitude to teacher
               effects, but they flow through improved information and
               assistance more than cognitive or noncognitive skill development.
