  pages     = "82--108",
  abstract  = "In the origins of sociology, ``social control'' served as a
               central concept both for relating sociology to social philosophy
               and for analyzing total societies. In its classical sense, it
               referred to the capacity of a social group to regulate itself.
               The concept supplied a basis for integration of theory and
               research until the 1930s. While the traditional usage of social
               control has persisted, the term has been redefined to mean either
               socialization or social repression. Either the classical meaning
