  author    = "Martin, Richard W",
  journal   = "Journal of urban economics",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  50,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "52--76",
  abstract  = "The spatial mismatch hypothesis suggests that a shift in the
               spatial distribution of jobs within a metropolitan area away from
               black residential areas will hurt their labor market outcomes. In
               the long run, this conclusion is true only if black residents are
               unable to adjust their residential locations in response to the
               employment shifts. This study uses information on employment and
               population shifts within American metropolitan areas from 1970 to
               1990 to measure the extent to which employment opportunities
               shifted away from black residents and to determine whether the
