  journal   = "Journal of public economics",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  92,
  number    = "8-9",
  pages     = "1821--1832",
  abstract  = "This article explores some conceptual issues in the study of
               well-being using the traditional economic approach of inferring
               preferences solely from choice behavior. We argue that choice
               behavior alone can never reveal which situations make people
               better off, even with unlimited data and under the maintained
               hypothesis of 100\% rational choice. Ancillary assumptions or
               additional forms of data such as happiness measures are always
               needed. With such ancillary assumptions and additional data,
               however, the use of revealed preference to study well-being can
               be significantly improved, so that the choices people make can
