  volume    =  126,
  pages     = "86--103",
  abstract  = "In many democracies, increasing political competition has changed
               the traditional party system and brought new parties into the
               political arena. This study examines whether the presence of
               politicians from new parties affects the size of government
               (measured by public spending and tax revenue). The study focuses
               on Colombian municipalities, where new parties have been numerous
               and successful in recent years. Regression discontinuity
               estimates show that public spending and tax revenues are
               significantly higher in municipalities governed by a mayor from a
               new party. Using information about local politics and the
               features of the new parties, I argue that this result is not
