  volume    =  34,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "491--519",
  abstract  = "Arbitrage costs are usually treated as a mere footnote in formal
               analyses of bimetallism. At the same time, recent empirical
               research has demonstrated their key importance, since they
               produced a ``bimetallic band''. This paper provides the first
               model of bimetallism that takes this explicitly into account and
               uses it to explain a number of stylized features of the French
               bimetallic experience 1850-1870. First, the model explains the
               association between the location of the price ratio within its
               band and the nature (either cross or joint) of specie flows.
               Second, it explains the correlation between bimetallic exchange
               rates and the bimetallic ratio. And third, it explains the
               two-humps distribution of the bimetallic ratio. This analysis
