  pages     = "554--567",
  abstract  = "The efficient decentralized provision of public goods requires
               that special interest groups, such as municipal unions, do not
               exercise undue influence on the outcome of municipal elections
               and local fiscal policies. We develop a new political economy
               model in which a union can endorse one of the candidates in a
               local election. A politician that prefers an inefficiently large
               public sector can, therefore, win an election if the union can
               provide sufficiently strong support during the campaign. We have
               assembled a unique data set that is based on union endorsements
               that are published in leading local newspapers. Our empirical
               analysis focuses on municipal elections in the 150 largest cities
               in the U.S. between 1990 and 2012. We find that challengers
