  publisher = "inderscienceonline.com",
  abstract  = "Employing a Hausman-Taylor instrument variable (HT-IV) estimator
               to data from 558 microfinance institutions (MFIs) in 80
               developing countries for the period from 2002 to 2007, this paper
               provides empirical evidence for a positive impact of a country's
               external governance quality and outcome on local microbanks'
               economic success in terms of profitability and sustainability.
               Evidence as well suggests a negative relationship between
               external governance and the microbanks' social success measured
               by the depth of outreach. In this context, our analysis reveals
               that a country's political stability, governance effectiveness,
               regulatory quality and rule of law are significant key elements
               of external governance affecting the MFIs' functional
