  pages     = "99--124",
  abstract  = "We use the 2013 federal government shutdown and a rich data set
               from an online personal finance website to study the effects of
               changes in income on changes in consumption for thousands of
               affected workers. The 2013 shutdown represented a significant and
               unanticipated income shock for federal government workers, with
               no direct effect on permanent income. We exploit both the
               differences between unaffected state government employees and
               affected federal employees as well as between federal employees
               required to remain at work and those required to stay at home to
               generate variation in income and available time. We find strong
               evidence for excess sensitivity of consumption patterns,
               violating the permanent income hypothesis. We demonstrate that
