  volume    =  44,
  number    = "S2",
  pages     = "799--813",
  abstract  = "In 1997, John Lott and David Mustard published an important paper
               in which they found that right‐to‐carry concealed weapons laws
               reduce violent crime. Although Lott and Mustard appear to do all
               possible variations of the analysis, a closer reading reveals
               that the study might suffer from several possibly important
               errors. I reestimate the model and check for incorrect functional
               form, omitted variables, and possible second‐order bias in the
               t‐ratios. Lott and Mustard's basic conclusions are generally
               robust with respect to these potential econometric problems.
