  number    =  4,
  pages     = "641--666",
  abstract  = "American Indian reservations are islands of poverty in a sea of
               wealth. Because this poverty cannot be explained solely by
               natural resource, physical, and human capital constraints,
               institutions are likely to be part of the explanation. One of the
               institutional variables is the sovereign power of tribes, which
               allows tribal governments to act opportunistically. The potential
               for such opportunistic behavior can thwart economic development
               if tribes are unable to make credible commitments to stable
               contract enforcement. One avenue for credible commitments is
               Public Law 280, which required some tribes to turn judicial
               jurisdiction over civil disputes to the states in which they
               reside. Using data for 1969–99, we find that per capita income
