  volume    =  130,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "135--165",
  abstract  = "We explore why insiders engage in informed trading, given the
               surprisingly small average insider returns reported in the
               literature and the potential costs involved. We begin by
               proposing a model of an insider's decision to engage in insider
               trading. We then empirically test the model's predictions using
               archival data of corporate insiders in Sweden. Consistent with
               the model, we find that less wealthy insiders are more likely to
               time their insider selling, and to sell in greater magnitudes,
               prior to abnormal price declines than wealthy insiders. We also
               find that less wealthy insiders with lower risk aversion as
