  volume    =  22,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "1--13",
  abstract  = "The patterns of decentralization of employment and population in
               American metropolitan areas are now well known to most urban
               economists. While much time and space has been devoted to the
               economic and social causes of suburbanization, understanding the
               nature of the process of decentralization is a prerequisite for
               the implementation of any government policy directed at the
