  pages     = "29--48",
  abstract  = "Community violence is recognized a significant public health
               problem. However, only a paucity of research has examined risk
               factors for community violence exposure across domains relevant
               to adolescents or using longitudinal data. This study examined
               youth aggressive behavior in relation to community violence
               exposure among a community epidemiologically defined sample of
               582 (45\% female) urban adolescents. Internalizing behaviors,
               deviant peer affiliation, and parental monitoring were examined
               as moderators of the association between aggressive behavior and
               exposure to community violence. For males with aggressive
               behavior problems and deviant peer affiliation or low parental
               monitoring, co-occurring anxiety symptoms protected against
