               (48.2 percent) report that they have either “a great deal” or
               “quite a bit” of confidence in the ability of legal authorities
               to respond to consumer fraud victimization. Bivariate
               correlations show that younger respondents, those with more
               formal education, recent fraud victims, and individuals inclined
               to take risks with their financial assets report lower levels of
               confidence. These findings persist in a multivariate context.
