  address   = "Los Angeles, CA",
  volume    =  62,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "645--668",
  abstract  = "Public policy in the area of youth crime has periodically shifted
               back and forth between punishment and child saving. Generally,
               scholars believe that public opinion and youth justice policies
               are linked. It is also believed that crime salience—fear and
               perceived risk of victimization—is linked to public opinion about
               youth sanctions. To test these widely held beliefs, this study
               examines public opinion about youth justice policies by exploring
               the impact of crime salience on public support for child saving
               versus youth punishment. This study expands on prior literature
               by also considering the public’s willingness to pay (WTP) for the
