  volume    =  31,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "905--933",
  abstract  = "Hot spots policing has been shown to be an effective strategy for
               reducing crime across a number of rigorous evaluations, but
               despite this strong body of research, there still exist gaps in
               our knowledge of how officers can best respond to hot spots. We
               report on a randomized experiment in Sacramento, California that
               begins to address these gaps by testing the recommendation from
               prior research that police officers randomly rotate between hot
               spots, spending about 15 min patrolling in each. Our results
               suggest significant overall declines in both calls for service
               and crime incidents in the treatment hot spots relative to the
