               rural communities in Australia",
  author    = "Jobes, Patrick C and Barclay, Elaine and Weinand, Herb and
               Donnermeyer, Joseph F",
  journal   = "The Australian \& New Zealand journal of criminology",
  publisher = "SAGE Publications",
  address   = "London, England",
  volume    =  37,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "114--140",
  abstract  = "This paper extends research on rural crime beyond North America
               by analysing associations between census measures of community
               structures and officially reported crime in rural New South Wales
               (Australia). It employs social disorganisation theory to examine
               variations in crime rates between different kinds of rural
               communities. A typology of rural communities was developed from
               cluster analysis of demographic, economic and social structural
               measures of rural local government areas (LGAs) in NSW. Six
               distinct types of rural communities were found to have unique
               crime characteristics. Structural measures were statistically
               associated with four types of crime. Overall, the findings
               support social disorganisation theory. Crime generally decreased
               across an urban-rural continuum, and more cohesive and integrated
