  volume    =  36,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "101--117",
  abstract  = "Crime hot spots, the roughly 5\% of locations responsible for
               half of all crime in a municipality, have almost exclusively been
               documented and further studied using offenses reported to the
               police. However, more than half of all UCR Part 1 offenses go
               unreported, and thus, the study of crime and place is largely a
               study of a relatively small percentage of cases. The current work
               assesses the effect of unreported crime on the number, size, and
               location of gun-related violent crime (GRVC) hot spots in
               Trenton, New Jersey. GRVCs reported to the police are
               supplemented with data from ShotSpotter, an acoustic
               gun-detection technology. Using nearest neighbor hierarchical
               clustering, this study finds that 25\% of hot spots would be
               unknown to the Trenton Police Department absent ShotSpotter.
