               urban rail supply, measured by vehicle-kilometers of the urban
               rail service, incorporating demand for urban rail travel, urban
               rail travel speed, conditions of alternative transportation
               modes, land use patterns, and socioeconomic conditions as
               explanatory variables. The model adopted a lag structure for
               urban rail supply in line with the planning horizon. It was then
               estimated using the Bayesian model averaging approach, which
               provided robust estimation results based on our multivariate time
               series data. The results showed that investments in the urban
               rail network in Tokyo were primarily driven by the increase in
               demand for urban rail travel. In effect, demand growth in the
               Tokyo rail network has clearly translated to supply growth
