               of repeat victimization was higher than that expected on the
               basis of statistical likelihood. Interestingly, the time course
               of repeat victimization conformed to an exponential model. Two
               alternative hypotheses are presented to attempt to explain this
               relationship. One highlights the increased vulnerability of a
               household following an initial burglary; the other attempts to
               explain the variation in repeat victimization in terms of
               characteristics of the offender. Other findings indicate a clear
