  volume    =  18,
  pages     = "aae053",
  abstract  = "Abstract This article presents results from a systematic review
               and meta-analysis of problem-oriented policing (POP). The results
               show an overall 33.8\% relative reduction in crime/disorder in
               treatment groups relative to controls, which adds to evidence
               that POP is an effective strategy that police leaders should
               adopt. There is, however, a great deal of variation in effect
               sizes, and moderator analyses were conducted to examine when POP
               may work best. Preliminary findings suggest POP may have larger
               impacts when responses are broader and involve more partner
               agencies/groups, when more of the agency is involved in the
               program, and when targeting property crime and disorder.
