  pages     = "107--121",
  abstract  = "General multilevel opportunity theory draws from a range of
               existing opportunity theories that collectively account for
               variation in criminal opportunity across various levels of
               analysis. The theory, which is inclusive in its conceptualization
               of criminal opportunity, specifies macro- and micro-influences
               and their interactive effects. There is a growing body of
               multilevel victimization research that supports the propositions
               of the theory. To date, however, general multilevel opportunity
