  abstract  = "Recent research has used both routine activity/lifestyle
               frameworks and self‐control theory to explain victimization. Thus
               far, combined tests of these theories have focused on offending
               populations and street crime victimization. Whether these
               frameworks also explain exposure to and likelihood of nonviolent
               victimization (e.g., fraud) in general‐population samples remains
               an open empirical question. Building on prior work, we assess the
               independent effects of routine consumer activities (i.e., remote
               purchasing) and low self‐control on the likelihood of fraud
               targeting and victimization. Using a representative sample of 922
