  volume    =  53,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "69--90",
  abstract  = "This study investigates the relationship between weather and
               crime in Barranquilla, Colombia, a city in the Torrid Zone, which
               in contrast to more commonly studied temperate zones is hot and
               humid year-round. Our analysis is based on daily variations in
               four weather variables (temperature, relative humidity,
               precipitation, and wind speed) and two indicators of criminal
               activity, namely, homicides and interpersonal violence. To help
               identify statistical links, we add controls for temporal
               variables. Using count data models in the estimations, we do not
               find any statistically significant relationship between weather
               patterns and homicides. However, we find that weather can be an
