  volume    =  19,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "164--180",
  abstract  = "Crime prevention initiatives are often conceptualized working at
               primary-secondary-tertiary (PST) levels. Primary prevention
               efforts address the underlying social, economic, and physical
               environmental conditions that generate crime; secondary
               prevention efforts focus on people, places, and social conditions
               that are at high risk of crime; whereas tertiary prevention
               efforts are directed toward already existing and specific crime
               problems. This article discusses the uses of the ambient
               population (a 24-hr average estimate of the population present in
               a spatial area) to better inform crime prevention initiatives
               within the PST framework. Though the results indicate the ambient
               population has utility for all three levels of crime prevention,
               the most immediate use is in tertiary prevention to better
               understand the nature of areas with a current crime problem. This
