               proclivities to use crime to accomplish ends, and different
               beliefs about society's fairness, are likely to respond to
               different incentives and disincentives that are derived from
               strain and neoclassical deterrence theories. Our analysis
               indicates that the crime control policies typically recommended
               by adherents of both theories are often logically invalid, given
               the premises upon which they are supposedly based. For example,
               our analysis suggests why punishment strategies like “three
               strikes and you're out” and “entitlement strategies” such as
