               p<.001), indicating a gradual adoption of World Health
               Organization-recommended terminology. 5,395 tweets were randomly
               selected from 9 days, 4 weeks apart and coded using a tri-axial
               coding scheme. To track tweet content and to test the feasibility
               of automated coding, we created database queries for keywords and
               correlated these results with manual coding. Content analysis
               indicated resource-related posts were most commonly shared
               (52.6\%). 4.5\% of cases were identified as misinformation. News
               websites were the most popular sources (23.2\%), while government
               and health agencies were linked only 1.5\% of the time. 7/10
               automated queries correlated with manual coding. Several Twitter
