  publisher = "Informa UK Limited",
  address   = "United States",
  volume    =  32,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "85--97",
  abstract  = "Abstract While the contemporary systemic model of neighborhood
               crime has emphasized the regulatory capacities that are imbedded
               in the structures of local community relational networks, very
               little attention has been paid to the dynamics of social control
               that are exercised through these linkages. This is a very
               important consideration, given recent reformulations of this
               framework as a multilevel model. This paper suggests that the
               social capital model of James Coleman (1990) can serve as an
               excellent theoretical basis for such an extension and examines
               the degree to which neighborhood networks serve as mechanisms of
               social control for a sample of Oklahoma City residents. Evidence
