  journal   = "Journal of environmental psychology",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  84,
  number    =  101914,
  pages     =  101914,
  abstract  = "The present research addresses tools that could help reduce
               littering in society. Four interventions were tested which, based
               on different processes, should reduce littering: monetary
               information, the depicted injunctive norm, watching eyes and a
               nature picture. To test these interventions, a randomized
               controlled trial (RCT) involving 440 community building's waste
               disposal areas (N = 71,155) was conducted in Vienna. Littering
               was assessed before the intervention, 24–48 h after, and again
               seven weeks after the intervention. Results show that the
               financial intervention (monetary information) hardly had any
               effect on littering whereas the norm-based intervention (depicted
               injunctive norm) led to more littering compared to the control
               and in particular, the nature picture. In contrast, the
               reputation-based intervention (watching eyes) and ecology-based
               intervention (nature picture) reduced littering over time by
               4.7\%. Thus, interventions based on implicit and soft appeals to
               reputation and ecology are more effective in fostering clean
