               theories provide a useful framework for evaluating networking as
               a spatial strategy and conceptualizing power relations within
               networks. We contend that forming spaces of engagement through
               scaled networks constituted by strong and weak ties is an
               essential means by which actors obtain information and leverage
               resources necessary to build and maintain urban community
               gardens. However, we also find that these networks contain power
               hierarchies that shape the conditions for participation in the
               networks. Actors with fewer resources and lesser political clout
               are compelled to conform to the interests of powerful actors. In
               employing social network theories to analyze urban community
               garden formation, this paper aims to achieve greater theorization
               and understanding of the complexities embedded in community
               garden development.",
