               survey of secondary school pupils in three very different schools
               in south London (n=1590). The focus is on the experience of
               robbery in school and on the journey to and from school;
               ‘experience’ includes those who were personally ‘robbed’ when
               they were alone, as well as those who were with others when they
               were ‘robbed’. The associations between this experience and
               perceptions of safety and safe behaviours are explored. The
               inherently problematic nature of this kind of survey methodology
               with children is highlighted, with reference to other well-known
               surveys in the United Kingdom. The survey found that overall 21.6
               per cent of pupils had experienced a robbery, of whom 7.9 per
               cent were alone at the time and 13.7 per cent were with a person
               who was robbed; 3.3 per cent of the whole sample reported that
               they had been injured. The article concludes that better
               practical use could be made of such data, especially if it was
               more routinely collected electronically and used in planning a
               response at the school level.",
  month     =  may,
  year      =  2012,
  keywords  = "",
  language  = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{DeKeseredy2009-Toward_A_Gendered_Second_Generation_CPTED_For_Preventing_Woman_Abuse_In_Rural_Communities-..-ki,
  title     = "Toward a gendered Second Generation {CPTED} for preventing woman
