  journal   = "American sociological review",
  publisher = "SAGE Publications",
  volume    =  69,
  number    =  6,
  pages     = "882--903",
  abstract  = "Robert Putnam comprehensively analyzes the multidimensional
               nature of social capital and makes a persuasive argument for its
               relevance to various community social problems, including violent
               crime. However, systematic empirical evaluations of the links
               between the multiple dimensions of social capital and violence
               are limited by the lack of adequate measures. Using data from the
               Social Capital Benchmark Survey, the authors model the
               relationships between several dimensions of social capital and
               homicide rates for 40 U.S. geographic areas. Their findings show
               that many forms of social capital highlighted in the literature
               as having beneficial consequences for communities are not related
               to homicide rates. Two dimensions of social capital, social trust
               and social activism, do exhibit significant associations with
               homicide rates, net of other influences. However, in the latter
               case, the relationship is positive, and in both cases,
               simultaneous equation models suggest that these dimensions of
               social capital are consequences as well as causes of homicide.
