  pages     = "1133--1148",
  abstract  = "ABSTRACTEthnic minority officers continue to be underrepresented
               across UK police forces. Further, some ethnic minority groups
               consistently report lower levels of confidence in police compared
               to their White British counterparts. Although there is consensus
               that a more ethnically representative police service is a good
               idea, there is limited evidence in the UK on how the public
               perceives officers of different ethnic appearance, and how this
               relates to trust, confidence and legitimacy. This paper presents
               findings from an online experiment (n?=?260) exploring how ethnic
               appearance affects perceptions of police. Our findings offer rare
               empirical support for a more ethnically representative police
               force. First, across respondents, we found that Black officers
               were perceived significantly more favourably than White or Asian
