  volume    =  79,
  number    =  101896,
  pages     =  101896,
  abstract  = "Purpose Prominent among police reform policy discussions is a
               focus on recruitment (particularly of BIPOC officers), as well as
               a reorientation of policing away from a “warrior” mindset. This
               study examines levels of militarization found in police
               recruitment videos to determine whether organizational and
               environmental variables predict the themes advertised to
               potential applicants. Methods Using the findings of a content
               analysis of a sample of United States police recruiting videos,
               principal components analysis is conducted to observe whether
               measures of community-oriented policing load on distinct
               components from measures of militarization, with results used to
               calculate a militarization score for each video. The study then
               examines predictors of, and protective factors against, high
               video militarization scores using ordinal logistic regression.
               Results Agencies that received federal COPS grants for
               community-oriented policing, and that have collaborative
               problem-solving integrated into officers' evaluations are
               significantly less likely to advertise highly militarized
               messages to potential applicants. Conclusion COPS grants are
               helpful in reducing militarized themes in recruiting videos,
               which may deter heavily “warrior”-minded applicants from
               applying. Agencies must also ensure that community-oriented
               philosophy is converted to actionable practice, and that these
               practices are showcased in recruiting materials to reflect the
               skills required for modern policing.",
