  volume    =  10,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "385--397",
  abstract  = "Food consumption constitutes behaviour that is carried out on a
               daily basis. The risks and benefits associated with such
               behaviours are often small, and the time and effort people are,
               consequently, willing to invest in behavioural decisions are
               limited. Instead, experience, in the form of an integrated
               evaluation of past behaviours, may serve as a predictor for
               future behaviour, allowing reasonable choices to be made with
               limited effort. This paper discusses the characteristics of daily
               occurring risks and suggests a model of how prior experience can
               accumulate and play a role in acting while exposed to daily risk.
