  volume    =  40,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "131--150",
  abstract  = "This article compares Beccaria’s and Situational Crime
               Prevention’s (SCP) claims across six dimensions. Both
               perspectives question harsh penalties, embrace crime reduction as
               a goal, and view some individuals as possessing agency and
               rationality. The latter two points distinguish them from most
               other criminological theories that are not focused on crime
               reduction and downplay offenders’ rationality. Both approaches
               have also been criticized for ignoring the root causes of crime
               in society. Importantly though, the approaches also differ. The
               Classical School and SCP are usually differentiated from
               positivistic approaches in their assumption of offender agency.
