  volume    =  29,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "273--293",
  abstract  = "Objectives Using data from a nationally representative survey of
               adolescents in Finland this research examined the influence of
               spending time in public settings on the risk of physical assault
               and robbery victimization. Methods Binary and multinomial
               regression models were estimated to disaggregate associations
               between hours spent in public settings and characteristics of the
               victimization incident. The amount of causality/spuriousness in
               the association was examined using a method of situational
               decomposition. Results Our findings indicate that: (1) an active
               night life (any time after 6 pm) has a strong effect on
               victimization for boys, whereas much of the association between
