  abstract  = "Geographically targeting law enforcement at drug hot spots is a
               common response to drug problems, but because they are generated
               with police data, they only reflect what the police already know
               about narcotics crime. In this study, we illustrate the
               importance of using multiple data sets to characterize the
               micro-spatial distribution of illicit drug events in Seattle,
               Washington, by examining and comparing the Seattle Fire
               Department’s Emergency Medical Services (EMS) calls and Seattle
               Police Department’s crime incidents in 2004. We find that both
               EMS calls and police incidents indicate illicit drug use is
               concentrated at a small number of street segments, yet their
