  volume    =  74,
  number    =  101806,
  pages     =  101806,
  abstract  = "Purpose: The aim of this review was to estimate the effect of
               COVID-19-related restrictions (i.e., stay at home orders,
               lockdown orders) on reported incidents of domestic violence.
               Methods: A systematic review of articles was conducted in various
               databases and a meta-analysis was also performed. The search was
               carried out based on conventional scientific standards that are
               outlined in the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Review
               and Meta-Analysis Protocols (PRISMA-P) and studies needed to meet
               certain criteria. Results: Analyses were conducted with a random
               effects restricted maximum likelihood model. Eighteen empirical
               studies (and 37 estimates) that met the general inclusion
               criteria were used. Results showed that most study estimates were
               indicative of an increase in domestic violence post-lockdowns.
               The overall mean effect size was 0.66 (CI: 0.08-1.24). The
               effects were stronger when only US studies were considered.
