               whether there are reasons to use one in favor of the others; and
               (3) apply the measures to a real-case data to further understand
               the concentration phenomenon. Using the crime data of Cincinnati,
               we compare four commonly used social science measures of
               concentration: Gini, Simpson, Shannon, and Decile indices. For
               some purposes, the measures are interchangeable, while for other
               purposes the measures may suggest different interpretations for
               the same set of data. This paper is the first quantitative
