               from a sample of 749 Turkish women, the current study applies the
               routine activity theory (RAT) framework, focusing on target
               suitability conceptualised as VIVA (value, inertia, visibility,
               and accessibility), to cybercrime victimisation, including
               cyberstalking, computer hacking, and credit card identity theft.
