               from the literature. The model is then estimated using
               self-report data from a three-state survey, focusing on four
               self-projected offenses and change in residence over a ten-year
               period. The results show no direct influences of mobility but
               they do indicate the presence of a fairly general indirect effect
               through the intervening linkage of moral commitment to
               conventional norms. Thus, in general, mobility appears to reduce
