  journal   = "Journal of contemporary criminal justice",
  publisher = "SAGE Publications",
  volume    =  35,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "142--160",
  abstract  = "In this paper we seek to identify whether the relationship
               between health disparities and crime occurs at a micro geographic
               level. Do hot spot streets evidence much higher levels of mental
               and physical illness than streets with little crime? Are
               residents of crime hot spots more likely to have health problems
               that interfere with their normal daily activities? To answer
               these questions, we draw upon a large National Institutes of
               Health study of a sample of hot spots and non-hot spots in
               Baltimore, Maryland. This is the first study we know of to report
               on this relationship, and accordingly we present unique
               descriptive data. Our findings show that both physical and mental
               health problems are much more likely to be found on hot spot
               streets than streets with little crime. This suggests that crime
