  volume    =  51,
  number    = "3-4",
  pages     = "359--369",
  abstract  = "The current study presents a case in which adolescent
               prosociality is lower in neighborhoods with greater physical
               disorder. Current theory provides two interpretations for such a
               pattern: (1) that disorder signals a threatening environment and
               discourages prosociality (``broken windows theory''); (2) that
               disorder and low prosociality are both symptoms of a weak
               community (i.e., low collective efficacy). A survey of 642
               students from a small American city was combined with an
               assessment of the built environment to evaluate these two
               interpretations. Students were nested in 59 Census block groups.
               Multilevel models demonstrated that collective efficacy best
               explained variation in prosociality between neighborhoods, and
               that perceived collective efficacy best explained variation
