               for power dynamics involving the “superior” perpetrator
               controlling the “inferior” victim, effective responses from the
               victim’s community are essential. However, these same
               communities, especially poor minority ones, are often socially
               disorganized and find it challenging to address the complex
               problems that stem from poverty. This exploratory study observes
               how a Cape Verdean community in the Northeast responded to
               racially motivated hate crimes that occurred there. Using the
               theoretical framework of social disorganization theory, social
