  pages     = "48--68",
  abstract  = "Fear of crime has become a major focus of attention both for
               policy makers and within the academic literature and although
               geography is considered relevant to fear of crime little is known
               about how it varies spatially. The present article applies
               multilevel modelling to a newly available geocoded version of the
               British Crime Survey to explore the feasibility of estimating
               fear of crime down to small area level across England. Five of
               the six models are considered sufficiently powerful in explaining
               the between-area variance to produce estimates. Estimates of fear
               of crime for these five crime types are produced (with 95 per
               cent credible intervals to indicate their likely precision) for
               small areas and local authorities in England and are placed in
               the public domain. The models also highlight, however, that most
               of the variance in individual's fear of crime is within rather
