  volume    =  9,
  number    =  1,
  pages     =  10,
  abstract  = "The spread of the coronavirus has led to containment policies in
               many places, with concomitant shifts in routine activities. Major
               declines in crime have been reported as a result. However, those
               declines depend on crime type and may differ by parts of a city
               and land uses. This paper examines burglary in Detroit, Michigan
               during the month of March, 2020, a period of considerable change
               in routine activities. We examine 879 block groups, separating
               those dominated by residential land use from those with more
               mixed land use. We divide the month into three periods:
               pre-containment, transition period, and post-containment.
