  abstract  = "Changes to how police forces in England and Wales are working to
               manage their public image in an environment of heightened
               accountability and transparency are explored. The locus of
               control of information shaping the portrayal of the police in the
               news media is discussed, as is the impact of the Freedom of
               Information Act (FOIA) 2000. The findings from case studies of
               two police forces in the East Midlands are reported. The case
               studies indicate that, whilst police and media cooperation is not
               a new phenomenon, UK police forces are becoming increasingly
               proactive, strategic and professionalised in their use of the
               news media. For their part, the media are dependent on the news
               ‘fed’ to them on a regular basis by police press relations units.
