@PHDTHESIS{Gennari2021-Engaging_Bystanders_To_Prevent_Sexual_Harassment_In_Public_Transportation_Learnings_From_An_Intervention_In_Mexico_City-..-jb,
  title     = "Engaging Bystanders to Prevent Sexual Harassment in Public
               Transportation: Learnings from an Intervention in Mexico City",
  author    = "Gennari, Floriza Freire",
  editor    = "Rimal, Rajiv N",
  publisher = "ProQuest Dissertations \& Theses",
  address   = "United States -- District of Columbia",
  abstract  = "The sexual harassment (SH) of women in public transportation is a
               widespread issue, yet research is sparse on how to prevent this
               type of violence from occurring. In three papers, this
               dissertation evaluates the impact of the Hazme el Paro! project,
               a quasi-experimental study that employed a bystander intervention
               approach to encourage witnesses to intervene in incidents of SH.
               The study was conducted in Mexico City, Mexico from 2014 to 2016.
               Papers 1 and 2 are exploratory studies of the psychosocial
               variables associated with perceived risk of SH and bystander
               willingness to intervene, while Paper 3 examines the effect of
               the Hazme el Paro! intervention.Paper 1 explored the correlates
               of SH risk perception, and the extent to which psychosocial
               variables moderated the relationship between experiencing or
               witnessing SH and the perceived risk of being sexually harassed.
               Among women, results suggest a positive association between risk
               perception and a) acceptance of SH norms as pervasive, b) the
               belief that most women disapprove of SH (injunctive norms), and
               c) witnessing or experiencing SH. The results indicate that risk
               perception is higher for women with a history of SH when
               victim-blaming beliefs are present. Among men, injunctive norms
               were negatively associated with risk perception. The findings
               suggest campaigns seeking to reduce SH in public transport should
               consider the role of risk perception antecedents in the framing
               of messages intending to motivate bystanders to action.Paper 2
               then seeks to examine the extent to which risk perception, along
               with other barriers and facilitators, affected willingness to
               intervene. As hypothesized, scoring high on self-efficacy to
               intervene attenuated the negative relationship between holding
               victim-blaming beliefs and the intention to help a victim of SH.
               Similarly, those with greater outcome expectations scored higher
               on willingness to intervene, despite their strong victim-blaming
               beliefs, than those who reported low outcome expectations. The
               findings suggest that promoting self-efficacy and outcome
               expectations may be a viable strategy for increasing bystander
               intention to intervene, specifically among those with stronger
               victim-blaming beliefs – the group least likely to express a
               desire to intervene.Paper 3 documents the extent to which the
               intervention mattered in significantly improving willingness to
               intervene and actual intervening behaviors. When examining the
               overall impact of the intervention bus route and controlling for
               changes in the comparison bus route as per the
               difference-in-difference method, the analyses showed statistical
               significance with the intervention being effective in increasing
               willingness to intervene at end-line. Based on end-line data
               only, male respondents in the intervention bus route were more
               likely to report prosocial bystander behaviors. Findings indicate
               a dose response effect; greater exposure to the intervention was
               associated with increased willingness to intervene (among men
               only) and bystander behavior. Paper 3 also sought to examine the
               pathway of influence of the intervention; results suggest outcome
               expectations and victim-blaming were pathways for both men and
               women.Overall, the findings from this dissertation suggest that a
               low-resource bystander intervention initiative in public
               transportation can indeed influence the intention of bystanders
               to step in and interrupt an incident of SH.",
