  number    =  2,
  pages     = "199--217",
  abstract  = "Since its national implementation in March 2014, the UK Domestic
               Violence Disclosure Scheme (also known as ‘Clare’s Law’) has
               enabled thousands of people in England and Wales to seek
               information from the police about whether their partner has a
               history of domestically abusive behaviours. Politicians have
               hailed the policy on the basis that it empowers people to make
               informed choices about their safety, thus representing a vital
               part of wider domestic violence reduction strategies. This, of
               course, is all dependent upon people knowing the policy exists;
               being able to apply to it; meeting the relevant criteria; there
               being information to disclose; and this being relayed to the
               applicant accordingly. Drawing on empirical research into the
               policy’s operation in one policing area, this paper highlights
