               London-wide data",
  author    = "Tiratelli, Matteo and Quinton, Paul and Bradford, Ben",
  journal   = "The British journal of criminology",
  publisher = "Oxford University Press (OUP)",
  volume    =  58,
  number    =  5,
  pages     = "1212--1231",
  abstract  = "In this article, we used ten years of police, crime and other
               data from London to investigate the potential effect of stop and
               search on crime. Using lagged regression models and a natural
               experiment, we show that the effect of stop and crime is likely
               to be marginal, at best. While there is some association between
               stop and search and crime (particularly drug crime), claims that
               this is an effective way to control and deter offending seem
               misplaced. We close the discussion by suggesting that, first, in
               a legal sense the key issue is that each and every stop should be
               justified in itself, not in that it has some putative wider
               effect on crime, and, second, in a sociological sense, our
               findings support the idea that stop and search is a tool of
               social control widely defined, not crime-fighting, narrowly
               defined.",
