               dominant explanatory framework for this relationship revolves
               around the idea that procedurally just practice increases
               people's positive connections to justice institutions. But there
               have been few assessments of the idea—central to procedural
               justice theory—that social identity acts as an important
               social-psychological bridge in this process. Our contribution in
               this paper is to examine the empirical links between procedural
               justice, social identity and legitimacy in the context of
               policing in Australia. A representative two-wave panel survey of
