  volume    =  32,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "191--213",
  abstract  = "Objectives Effects of place-based criminal justice interventions
               extend across both space and time, yet methodological approaches
               for evaluating these programs often do not accommodate the
               spatiotemporal dimension of the data. This paper presents an
               example of a bivariate spatiotemporal Ripley’s K-function, which
               is increasingly employed in the field of epidemiology to analyze
               spatiotemporal event data. Advantages of this technique over the
               adapted Knox test are discussed. Methods The study relies on x–y
               coordinates of the exact locations of stop-question-frisk (SQF)
