  volume    =  42,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "221--232",
  abstract  = "Public opinion scholarship has identified the media as a driving
               force behind decidedly negative public sentiment about crime and
               justice. We draw on this media cultivation framework to examine
               whether the highly publicized sexual abuse scandal within the
               Catholic Church impacted public opinion.Using data from a 2010
               CBS/New York Times national poll we investigate how exposure to
               news coverage detailing the abuse affected levels of public
               confidence in the Church’s ability to protect
               children.Contrasting with prior research, we uncovered a positive
               impact of media exposure. Catholics with greater media
               consumption about the scandal were significantly more confident
               in the Church’s ability to prevent sexual abuse. In addition,
