               crime rates and poor police-community relations. Survey
               respondents include 274 (51 per cent.) representatives of
               coloured ethnic minority groups (this includes 17 per cent, of
               West Indian origin or descent and 34 per cent, of Asian origin or
               * Ph.D. Visiting assistant professor, Department of Geography,
               University of California. 1 The contents of this newspaper were
               monitored for seven months prior to administering the household
               survey. For a range of themes, the numbers, lengths, and types of
               articles were recorded, together with any referenced
               neighbourhoods. This provided a basis for comparing crime news
               with both official crime statistics and popular images of
               deviance. In effecting such comparisons, content analysis is
               preferable to various other approaches to textual analysis,
               including analytical semantics, structural analysis and
               hermeneutic interpretation. Content analysis assumes a
               relationship between the frequency of a content and its import,
               and requires the division of a text into meaningful yet
