               distribution of crime over a city’s census tracts (CTs). A
               “funnel hypothesis” states that these daily flows are central for
               crime concentrations within a city. We present arguments for and
               against funneling prior to empirical analysis.MethodsA municipal
               transport agency in a large city in Eastern Canada surveyed
               66,100 households about daily trips for work, shopping,
               recreation, and school. This allowed us to link inflows of
               visitors to numbers of property and violent crimes for 506
