  pages     = "95--110",
  abstract  = "The courts of summary jurisdiction in Victoria have recently
               streamlined procedures for the hearing of guilty pleas:
               sentencing decisions are now determined largely on the basis of a
               brief summary of ‘facts’ presented by the prosecution. To assess
               the extent to which these summaries detail the harm suffered by
               victims we analysed 276 assault case summaries. The data indicate
               that summaries rarely report details of victim injury even where
               this information had been gathered by police. However, the data
               also suggest that disclosing details of victim injury makes
               little difference to sentencing and other decisions. This article
               outlines how existing procedures have allowed information about
