               major alternative and supplement to the criminal justice system.
               This article attempts to review what is known currently about the
               nature, extent, and effectiveness of community-based efforts to
               prevent residential crime. Included in this assessment are
               citizen actions to protect themselves, their property, and their
               neighborhood, as well as efforts to prevent crime through changes
               in the physical environmental and through innovations in
               community policing. The historical, theoretical, and empirical
               rationale for community crime prevention strategies are
               discussed, but primary attention is given to the results of
