  pages     = "1949--1954",
  abstract  = "During the past few decades, punitive crime policies have led to
               explosive growth in the United States prison population. Such
               policies have contributed to unprecedented incarceration rates
               for Blacks in particular. In this article, we consider an
               unexamined relationship between racial disparities and policy
               reform. Rather than treating racial disparities as an outcome to
               be measured, we exposed people to real and extreme racial
               disparities and observed how this drove their support for harsh
               criminal-justice policies. In two experiments, we manipulated the
