  address   = "Los Angeles, CA",
  volume    =  54,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "454--478",
  abstract  = "Objectives: The principal objective of the current study is to
               test the applicability of Tom Tyler’s process-based model of
               policing in China. A secondary objective of this research is to
               examine the internal consistency and discriminant validity of key
               composite constructs in Tyler’s model. Methods: Using survey data
               collected from approximately 1,000 residents in a Chinese city,
               ordinary least squares regression was employed to assess the
               direct and indirect (through legitimacy) effects of procedural
               justice, specific distributive justice, and police effectiveness
               on willingness to cooperate with the police. Results: The
               internal consistency and discriminant validity of key composites
               in Tyler’s original model appear questionable with the Chinese
               data. Procedural justice, just like in the West, plays a
               significant role in predicting Chinese views on police legitimacy
