               United States, but the mechanisms that underlie these disparities
               in victimization risk are not well understood. This study tests a
               mediation model developed from criminal opportunity theories that
               may explain the disparities. Using data from the Project on Human
               Development in Chicago Neighborhoods, the results show that Black
               and Hispanic adolescents were twice as likely as their White
               counterparts to be violently victimized, and these disparities
               remained after controlling for demographic characteristics and
