  journal  = "The British journal of criminology",
  volume   =  41,
  pages    = "595--617",
  abstract = "Recent research has been concerned with repeat victimization. In
              this paper, wefocus on another form of multiple
              victimization—multiple crime-type victimization (MCV); that is,
              the extent to which some households or persons are victims of more
              than one kind of offence over a given period. First, we discuss
              three features of our present understanding of multiple
              victimization—repetition over time, specifidty, and risk
              transmission. Second, we fit an appropriate multivariate
              statistical model to data taken from the 1992 British Crime Survey
              (BCS) in an effort to identify antecedents and correlates of MCV;
              and third, we discuss some of the implications of this analysis
              for a general under standing of crime victimization. From the
              earliest analyses of crime victimization surveys, 'multiple
              victimization' has invited attention. Understanding how and why
              some persons or households are victimized more frequendy than
              others offers the prospect of finding answers to questions about
              the causes and remedies of victimization 'which maybe easier to
              see if we look at multiple or recurrent victimization rather than
              occasional, sporadic or egregious events' (Sparks 1981: 763).
              Moreover, it is argued, the idea that crime victimization might be
              concentrated amongst particular places, persons, households or
              property promises considerable benefit to public policy if crime
              preventive and detective resources likewise can be targeted where
              and when they appear to be most needed (Pease 1998; Pease and
              Laycock 1996). The arrival, more than 20 years ago, of reasonably
              large sample surveys of household victimization provided an
              empirical opportunity to study multiple victimization yet, despite
              earlier research (Sparks et al. 1977; Hindelang et al. 1978;
              Nelson 1980; Sparks 1981), interest in investigating the multiple
              victimization waned and has only recendy revived (Farrell 1992).
              Even so, most recent attention has focused chiefly on one aspect
              of multiple victim ization—repeat victimization (RV) (see Pease
              1998). With repeat victimization, the individual is the victim of
              two or more crimes of the same type within same time period. Other
              observed phenomena of'multiple victimization' have received much
              less research attention. Yet these might also yield insight for
              theory or policy. More pressing still, though only cursorily
              attempted here, is a need for a conceptual framework which would
              help delineate the various aspects of multiple victimization and
              the possible links between them. As will become apparent, asking
              questions about multiple victimization raises issues which ought
              to be relevant for our understanding of the concept of crime",
  year     =  2001,
  keywords = ""
}

