               that every victimization might form a contagion-like pattern
               nearby in terms of both space and time. In this article, the
               near-repeat phenomenon is used to analyze the risk levels around
               hot spots. Utilizing a recent burglary dataset in N (a large city
               located in southeastern China), we examine the near-repeat
               phenomenon, the results of which we then use to test the
               contributions of hot spots. More importantly, we propose a
               temporal expanded near-repeat matrix to quantify the undulation
               of risk both before and after hot spots. The experimental results
               demonstrate that hot spots always form. Space-time areas of high
