  number    =  11,
  pages     = "2230--2255",
  abstract  = "Response‐effects research has shown that survey questions shape
               and channel public opinion. Our study examines the degree to
               which the variance in people's cognitions about and attitudes
               toward crime policy proposals is a methodological artifact and
               the degree to which it represents media effects. Four different
               forms of a survey were used to manipulate the order of the
               proposal (death penalty vs. rehabilitation) and criterion
               (cognition vs. attitude). Results show that experimental
               manipulations affected cognitions or attitudes only for the death
               penalty. Respondents who were asked the cognition measure first
               showed a high level of consistency between the net valence of
