               these resourceful ties at a disproportionate rate over time. On
               the other hand, we find that income has a robust association with
               the ability to retain ties initially providing advice and help.
               We interpret the latter findings as a temporal manifestation of
               network-based inequality. The maintenance gap between higher- and
               lower-SES Americans, we argue, can reinforce other social capital
