  volume    =  43,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "193--213",
  abstract  = "This study applies a symbolic interaction perspective to the
               investigation of smoking frequency and a person's desire to quit
               smoking cigarettes. Data derived from 485 Atlanta area adult
               smokers provide a diverse, community-based sample of married and
               single men and women, aged 18 to 70 years old with a range of
               income, education, and occupational experiences. Multiple
               regression was used to analyze the data in order to explore the
               influence of social demographic characteristics, social
               interaction, subjective assessments of health, self conceptions,
               and smoker identity on smoking frequency and quitting smoking.
               Findings include: (1) the relationship with a non-smoker and
               hiding smoking negatively impacted smoking frequency, while
               perceiving positive consequences from smoking has a positive
