  journal   = "Environment and behavior",
  publisher = "SAGE Publications",
  address   = "Los Angeles, CA",
  volume    =  40,
  number    =  6,
  pages     = "870--891",
  abstract  = "Two studies tested littering norm activation by trash can design.
               The first was a scenario study using a 4 (norm type: social
               injunctive vs. social descriptive vs. personal vs. control) × 2
               (activation type: explicit vs. implicit activation) between-group
               design, with judgments of a litterer as the dependent variable.
               Explicit norm activation was more effective than implicit
               activation. A field study subsequently tested the effect of
               personal norm activation on actual littering behavior, following
               a 2 (explicit activation: no vs. yes) × 2 (Implicit activation:
               no vs. yes) between-group design. Here, both explicit activation
               through a verbal prompt and implicit activation through design
               had significant effects, reducing the amount of litter by 50\%. A
               post hoc survey revealed significant effects of age and gender on
               the personal norm against littering. These findings helped
               explain the absence of norm activation effects in the youngest
               age group as found in the field study.",
