  volume    =  50,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "616--631",
  abstract  = "OBJECTIVES: Test the influence of darkness in the street robbery
               crime event alongside temperature. METHODS: Negative binomial
               regression models tested darkness and temperature as predictors
               of street robbery. Units of analysis were four 6-hr time
               intervals in two U.K. study areas that have different levels of
               darkness and variations of temperature throughout the year.
               RESULTS: Darkness is a key factor related to robbery events in
               both study areas. Traversing from full daylight to full darkness
               increased the predicted volume of robbery by a multiple of 2.6 in
               London and 1.2 in Glasgow. Temperature was significant only in
               the London study area. Interaction terms did not enhance the
               predictive power of the models. CONCLUSION: Darkness is an
