  journal   = "The Review of Economic Studies",
  publisher = "Oxford University Press (OUP)",
  volume    =  60,
  number    =  3,
  pages     =  531,
  abstract  = "This paper examines the reflection problem that arises when a
               researcher observing the distribution of behaviour in a
               population tries to infer whether the average behaviour in some
               group influences the behaviour of the individuals that comprise
               the group. It is found that inference is not possible unless the
               researcher has prior information specifying the compisition of
               reference groups. If this information is available, the prospects
               for inference depend critically on the population relationship
               between the variables defining reference groups and those
               directly affecting outcomes. Inference is difficult to
               implossible if these variables are functionally dependent or are
               statistically independent. The prospects are better if the
               variables defining reference groups and those directly affecting
               outcomes are moderately related in the population.",
