  pages     = "219--234",
  abstract  = "Faced with continuing fiscal constraints, a growing number of
               communities have sought to provide police services in
               nontraditional ways. While a considerable amount of previous
               research explores community opinion about the police, no previous
               work, to our knowledge, focuses on what residents think of these
               nontraditional models. We surveyed residents in four communities
               with alternative models of policing: a merged department, two
               agencies that contracts for services, and a regional agency. We
