  volume    =  117,
  number    =  6,
  pages     = "1155--1159",
  abstract  = "Knowles, Persico, and Todd (2001) present a model of police and
               motorist behavior in the context of vehicle searches and test it
               using data from Maryland. Their work marked a resurgence in
               interest on how to interpret purported evidence of statistical
               and racial discrimination. The main implication of the their
               model is that in the absence of racial discrimination, the
               proportion of searches yielding drugs (or “hit rate”) will be
