  volume    =  64,
  number    = "3-4",
  pages     = "391--405",
  abstract  = "Metropolitan areas in the United States are characterized by both
               geographic concentration in robbery rates, and racial segregation
               in residential patterns. We argue that these two phenomena are
               closely connected. Robberies typically involve incomplete
               information about the likelihood of victim resistance and
               offender violence. Geographic concentration in robbery rates can
               lead to segregation (in excess of levels that would emerge under
               neighborhood sorting by income) because robbers prey
