  volume    =  97,
  pages     = "15--39",
  abstract  = "We develop a model where, in the first stage, minority
               individuals have to decide whether or not they want to assimilate
               to the majority culture while, in the second stage, all
               individuals (both from the majority and the minority group)
               embedded in a network have to decide how much effort they exert
               in some activity (say education). We show that the more central
               minority agents are located in the social network, the more they
               assimilate to the majority culture. We also show that denser
               networks tend to favor assimilation so that, for example, it is
               easier to assimilate in a complete network than in a star-shaped
               network. We show that the subgame-perfect equilibrium is not
               optimal because there is not enough activity and assimilation. We
