  pages     = "761--796",
  abstract  = "The recent federal education bill, No Child Left Behind, requires
               states to test students in grades 3 to 8 each year and to judge
               school performance on the basis of these test scores. While
               intended to maximize student learning, there is little empirical
               evidence about the effectiveness of such policies. This study
               examines the impact of an accountability policy implemented in
               the Chicago Public Schools in 1996–1997. Using a panel of
               student-level, administrative data, I find that math and reading
               achievement increased sharply following the introduction of the
               accountability policy, in comparison to both prior achievement
               trends in the district and to changes experienced by other large,
