  volume    =  65,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "74--90",
  abstract  = "While residents receive similar benefits from many local
               government programs, only about one-third of all households have
               children in public schools. We argue that capitalization of
               school spending into house prices can encourage even childless
               residents to support spending on schools. We identify a proxy for
               the extent of capitalization—the supply of land available for new
               development—and show that towns in Massachusetts with little
               undeveloped land have larger changes in house prices in response
