  author    = "Witte, Kristof De and Geys, Benny",
  journal   = "Journal of urban economics",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  69,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "319--327",
  abstract  = "Provision of most public goods (e.g., health care, libraries,
               education, police, fire protection, utilities) can be
               characterized by a two-stage production process. In the
               first-stage, basic inputs (e.g., labor and capital) are used to
               generate service potential (e.g., opening hours, materials),
               which is then, in the second-stage, transformed into observed
               outputs (e.g., school outcomes, library circulation, crimes
               solved). As final outputs are also affected by demand-side
               factors, conflating both production stages likely leads to biased
               inferences about public productive (in)efficiency and its
               determinants. Hence, this paper uses a specially tailored, fully
               non-parametric efficiency model allowing for both outlying
               observations and heterogeneity to analyse efficient public good
               provision in stage one only. We thereby employ a dataset
               comprising all 290 Flemish public libraries. Our findings suggest
               that ideological stance of the local government, wealth and
