  volume    =  2,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "138--159",
  abstract  = "We study the design of enforcement mechanisms when enforcement
               resources are chosen ex ante and are inelastic ex post. Multiple
               equilibria arise naturally. We identify a new answer to the old
               question of why non-maximal penalties are used to punish moderate
               actions: “marginal” penalties are much more attractive in the
               Pareto inferior crime wave equilibrium. Specifically, although
               marginal penalties have both costs and benefits, the net benefit
