  journal   = "Eastern Economic Journal",
  publisher = "JSTOR",
  volume    =  10,
  pages     = "315--321",
  abstract  = "Following in the tradition in which crime is treated as an
               economic activity 2, 3, 4 this paper offers an economic analysis
               of specialized crime producers, commonly known as ``organized
               crime.'' This analysis contrasts with previous research which
               analyzed the practices, products and by-products of organized
               crime 1,6,8 without seriously attempting to explain why such an
               organization exists. Building on the basic premise that all
               criminals are concerned with maximizing profit and avoiding
               arrest, this paper argues that organized crime is distinguished
               by the fact that organized crime devotes more resources to insure
               that its members will not aid the police. This particular
               resource allocation is made not simply to make the policeman's
               job more difficult, but because it provides organized crime with
               a comparative advantage in the commission of certain crimes. The
               economics of this resource allocation predicts that organized
               crime will not dominate the production of all crimes and will, in
               fact, avoid crimes such as shoplifting and car theft. Further,
               the analysis explains why organized criminals may actively
               participate in crimes such as importing and wholesaling of
               heroin, while steering clear of a related crime such as the
               retailing of heroin.",
  year      =  1984
}

