  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  abstract  = "The use of risk assessment scores as a means of decreasing
               pretrial detention for low-risk, primarily poor defendants is
               increasing rapidly across the United States. Despite this, there
               is little evidence on how risk assessment scores alter criminal
               outcomes. Using administrative data from a large county in Texas,
               we estimate the effect of a risk assessment score policy on judge
               bond decisions, defendant pretrial detention, and pretrial
               recidivism. We identify effects by exploiting a large, sudden
               policy change using a regression discontinuity design. This
               approach effectively compares defendants booked just before and
               after the policy change. Results show that adopting a risk
               assessment score leads to increased release on non-financial bond
