  journal   = "The journal of human resources",
  publisher = "University of Wisconsin Press",
  volume    =  48,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "311--344",
  abstract  = "This paper uses a regression discontinuity design to examine the
               effects of geographically targeted business incentives on local
               labor markets. Unlike elsewhere in the United States, enterprise
               zone (EZ) designations in Texas are determined in part by a
               cutoff rule based on census block group poverty rates. Exploiting
               this discontinuity as a source of quasi-experimental variation in
               investment and hiring incentives across areas, I find that EZ
               designation has a positive effect on resident employment,
               increasing opportunities mainly in lower-paying industries. While
               business sitings spurred by the program are more geographically
               diffuse, EZ designation is associated with increases in home
               values.",
