  journal   = "Journal of monetary economics",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  68,
  pages     = "S68--S80",
  abstract  = "Internal and sovereign debt crises occur together and happen more
               frequently in economies with weak bankruptcy institutions. This
               paper provides a novel explanation. Internal crises arise because
               of the inability to liquidate private debtors when many default.
               In an optimal contract, a successful entrepreneur repays yet an
               unsuccessful one defaults and liquidates his assets. The bounds
               on liquidation generate, however, a second equilibrium where
               domestic borrowers default because others are also defaulting.
