  volume    =  108,
  number    =  504,
  pages     = "1324--1338",
  abstract  = "In many applications, it is of interest to study trends over time
               in relationships among categorical variables, such as age group,
               ethnicity, religious affiliation, political party and preference
               for particular policies. At each time point, a sample of
               individuals provide responses to a set of questions, with
               different individuals sampled at each time. In such settings,
               there tends to be abundant missing data and the variables being
               measured may change over time. At each time point, one obtains a
               large sparse contingency table, with the number of cells often
               much larger than the number of individuals being surveyed. To
               borrow information across time in modeling large sparse
               contingency tables, we propose a Bayesian autoregressive tensor
               factorization approach. The proposed model relies on a
               probabilistic Parafac factorization of the joint pmf
               characterizing the categorical data distribution at each time
               point, with autocorrelation included across times. Efficient
               computational methods are developed relying on MCMC. The methods
               are evaluated through simulation examples and applied to social
               survey data.",
