  volume    =  54,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "953--983",
  abstract  = "This paper evaluates the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA), a law
               mandating that banks help meet the credit needs of lower income
               households and neighborhoods. To measure the effect of the law on
               lending to targeted groups since 1994, I take advantage of
               discontinuous targeting rules and abrupt changes in target
               status. On average, the CRA appears to have had little impact on
               mortgage lending, even during the mid-2000s, when lending to
               lower income areas nevertheless soared. I do find a significant
               effect during the late 1990s and early 2000s in large
               metropolitan areas, when and where the CRA may have been most
               binding. I use this episode to test the effect of the CRA on
               overall mortgage availability—that is, lending by both regulated
               and unregulated institutions. The results are consistent with the
               notion that government intervention in credit markets may be
               justified on the grounds that information externalities exist and
