  volume    =  38,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "721--740",
  abstract  = "Abstract Recidivism rates are a growing concern due to the high
               cost of imprisonment and the high rate of ex-prisoners returning
               back to prison. One policy-relevant and potentially important
               determinant of recidivism is the composition of peer inmates. In
               this paper, we study the role of peer effects within a
               correctional facility using data on almost 80,000 individuals
               serving time in Georgia. We exploit randomness in
               peer-composition over time within prisons to identify effects of
               peers on recidivism rates. We find no evidence of peer effects
