  author    = "Gray, Thomas R",
  journal   = "Journal of law, economics, \& organization",
  publisher = "Oxford University Press (OUP)",
  volume    =  35,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "422--453",
  abstract  = "Abstract State supreme court justices are often the final
               arbiters of cases in their jurisdictions. Yet, in states that
               grant governors the power to selectively reappoint supreme court
               justices, justices’ independence is limited. These governors are
               able to monitor justices’ decisions and are empowered to remove
               justices whose jurisprudence conflicts with the governor’s
               preferences. This power gives governors substantial influence
