  volume    =  38,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "479--510",
  abstract  = "Abstract It is well understood that when firms receive favorable
               treatment from the government because of their political
               connections and not necessarily their economic merits, they may
               operate inefficiently while enjoying market advantages over their
               unconnected peers. However, just how firms respond to the
               sustained removal of their political connections has not been
               carefully studied. This article evaluates an unanticipated reform
               in China that removed government-related personnel from
               independent directorships of publicly listed companies. Our
