  volume    =  25,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "237--255",
  abstract  = "Violent injury varies widely across England and Wales as does the
               price of alcohol. While the links between alcohol consumption and
               violence are well established in the medical and epidemiological
               literature, a causal link is questionable. This paper cuts
               through the causative argument by reporting a link between the
               general price of alcohol and violence-related injury across the
               economic regions of England and Wales. It examines the influence
               of the real price of alcohol and identifies an 'April effect'
               that coincides with the annual uprating of alcohol prices for
               excise duties, on violence-related injuries recorded at Emergency
