  volume    =  78,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "203--241",
  abstract  = "How costly is the poor governance of market intermediaries? Using
               unique trade level data from the stock market in Pakistan, we
               find that when brokers trade on their own behalf, they earn
               annual rates of return that are 50-90 percentage points higher
               than those earned by outside investors. Neither market timing nor
               liquidity provision by brokers can explain this profitability
               differential. Instead we find compelling evidence for a specific
               trade-based “pump and dump” price manipulation scheme: When
               prices are low, colluding brokers trade amongst themselves to
               artificially raise prices and attract positive-feedback traders.
               Once prices have risen, the former exit leaving the latter to
               suffer the ensuing price fall. Conservative estimates suggest
               these manipulation rents can account for almost a half of total
               broker earnings. These large rents may explain why market reforms
               are hard to implement and emerging equity markets often remain
               marginal with few outsiders investing and little capital raised.",
  month     =  oct,
  year      =  2005,
  language  = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Beck2000-Finance_And_The_Sources_Of_Growth-..-vm,
