               Navarre, Catalonia, and Melgueil) and north (Burgundy, Blois,
               Champagne, Nevers, and Picardy), the writers and sources on
               monetary matters (covering pecuniary obligations, profits,
               taxation, and wealth), and a conclusion drawing together the
               differences and similarities in the programs of the above
               regions. An appendix of selected documents, a bibliography, an
               index of coinage charters, decretals, and regulations, a good
               general index, and several plates of selected coins follow the
               text. Even though medieval enactments professed a dedication to
               just minting practices, Bisson shows the problems that arose by
               having 60 to 80 mints in France alone, of which approximately 20
               were royal. Periodic debasements or mutations such as
               counterfeiting bronze to resemble silver, frequent allocations of
               ``hush money,'' and other illegal practices aimed at increasing
               profits led to popular discontent. One method chosen to insure
               that governments would desist from such practices was the
               promulgation of money-taxes. Begun earliest in Normandy, in the
               late eleventh or early twelfth century, they were issued
               triennially on hearths at a rate of 12d. per hearth. When these
               taxes spread to other regions different scales were established.
               In Capetian France, for example, they were promulgated either as
               a lump sum collected from a given locale or as a percentage tax
               on muids (from L. modius, which Bisson translates inappropriately
               as ``measure'') of wine, wheat, oats, and so on. Beginning
               somewhat later in the south, they were often subject to popular
               approval and sometimes incorporated ceremonial oaths to preserve
               the coinage for life in proper weight and alloy. This idea of
               perpetuating the legitimate coinages, coupled with those of
               preventing the arbitrary exercise of monetary prerogatives, of
               confirming the coinages upon earlier medieval regulations, of
               protecting rulers and public alike from counterfeiting and other
               abusive practices, of stabilizing coinages in commercially
               realistic ratios, and of widening the circulation of stronger
               coinages, were the underlying reasons for all of the enactments
               issued irrespective of location.",
  month     =  jun,
  year      =  1980,
  language  = "en"
}

