@ARTICLE{Rothenberg1984-Markets_Values_And_Capitalism_A_Discourse_On_Method-..-af,
  title     = "Markets, values and capitalism: A discourse on method",
  author    = "Rothenberg, Winifred B",
  journal   = "The Journal of Economic History",
  publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
  volume    =  44,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "174--178",
  abstract  = "As to ``ahistorical behavioralism,'' a parable will serve here.
               Some years ago, the Massachusetts Institute of Technology had an
               alternative program for freshmen integrating all their course
               work around one theme. In the year of which I speak that theme
               was the Moon. Not only aeronautical engineers, astrophysicists,
               and geologists, but also social scientists, medievalists, and
               musicologists participated together in this focus on the Moon.
               The economist in the seminar that year decided to test the
               proposition, repeatedly implied but never operationally
               formulated, that the moon influences human behavior. He ran a
               regression, over the entire period for which New York City police
               statistics are available, of crimes against persons on the phases
               of the moon. The results were unequivocal: crimes of violence
               correlate highly and significantly with the gibbous phase of the
               moon. Even in New York City where one can hardly ever see the
               moon. The medievalist said, ``That is very interesting, sir, but
               you should know that your regression tells us nothing new about
               the moon; it only tells us what economists mean by Truth!'' To an
               ever growing extent that is what economists and economic
               historians mean by truth: a hypothesis, a model, and a
               verification procedure. Is that ``ahistorical behavioralism''? If
               so, what is the alternative? Assuming that they believe at all in
               hypothesis testing, what kind of evidence would my critics have
               preferred? The Bible promises that ``the sun shall not smite thee
               by day nor the moon by night.'' Shakespeare's reptilian Richard
               III confesses that he is ``governed by the watery moon,'' and
               Othello blames everything, in the end, on ``the very error of the
               moon.! She comes more nearer earth than she was wont/ and makes
               men mad.'' But is such testimony evidence, and if so, of what? Of
               the association between the moon and aberrant human behavior? I
               think not. No more than a thousand years of astronomical
               speculation was evidence of the existence of Ptolemy's epicycles.
               Rather it is evidence of the persistence of an idea through time.
               The regression, on the other hand, if properly specified, cuts
               through to the observable correlates of the phenomenon beneath.
               The question of causation, of HOW the moon pulls the tides and
               makes men mad (to say nothing of the metaphysical question of
               WHY) is properly left until after it has been established that it
               in fact does. And then, that the regression procedure may not, in
               point of fact, be the appropriate one for questions of How and
               Why does not diminish the contribution it made to the question it
               was designed to answer, the hypothesis it was designed to test.
               The hypothesis my procedures were designed to test is similarly
               limited: that we can observe in the behavior of an economic
               system itself, evidence of the micro-level decision making that
               propels a rural economy into the very act of generating rapid and
               self-sustaining growth.",
  month     =  mar,
  year      =  1984
}

