  pages     = "152--163",
  abstract  = "In his autobiography, Cheerful Yesterdays, Thomas Wentworth
               Higginson, looking back on the long crusade that ended with the
               abolition of Negro bondage in the United States, declared: “The
               anti-slavery movement was not strongest in the educated classes,
               but was primarily a people's movement, based on the simplest
               human instincts and far stronger … in the factories and
               shoe-shops than in the pulpits and colleges.” Few people have
