  number    =  3,
  pages     = "791--792",
  abstract  = "evolution of monetary policy in the United States from 1896 to
               the present. The book is intended primarily as a supplement for
               undergraduate courses in money and banking, but would be useful
               reading for anyone interested in an overview of U.S. monetary
               policy in the twentieth century. It describes the events,
               theories, and personalities which have shaped policy, and it
               exposes the failures of policy. Finally, Degen offers the
               following prescription for the conduct of policy: ``It is
               important . . . to keep in mind that we live in a changing
               environment. . . . Policy-making based on experience as
               interpreted by theoretical understanding, yet aware of
               institutional changes and open to correction in the light of new
               knowledge, may be the best approach'' (p. 197). The book begins
               with a description of the problems of the pre-Federal Reserve
               gold standard era—deflation, an inelastic currency, and financial
               panic. The Federal Reserve was created to alleviate these
               problems, and Degen illustrates the influence of then current
               economic theory on the Fed's design. Subsequent chapters show
               that policy changes—new regulations, changes in Fed procedures,
               and so on—were made in response to unsatisfactory economic
               performance, and were shaped by new economic theory. The Fed's
               switch to a strategy of targeting monetary growth in the late
               1970s is one example. Dissatisfaction with higher rates of
               inflation led the Federal Reserve System to adopt a monetarist
               strategy. I found material in two chapters particularly well
               presented. The fourth chapter covers the Great Depression years,
               1929-1939. The second half of this chapter focuses on the New
               Deal banking reforms and changes in the Fed's structure and
               personnel. One section, ``Keynes and Some Central Bankers,''
               describes the influence of Keynes's General Theory and its
               implications for monetary and fiscal policy on Marriner Eccles
               and Lauchlin Currie, and is quite interesting. And in Chapter 8,
               which covers the period 1965 to 1969, there is an excellent
               discussion of the Fed's ``money market'' strategy and how rapid
               money growth and inflation exposed the flaws in it. The book
               focuses exclusively on domestic monetary policy. International
               objectives and constraints on policy are mentioned only when
               absolutely necessary. There is no discussion of the collapse of
               the Bretton Woods system, for example, or of the different
               constraints imposed on monetary policy by fixed and flexible
               exchange rates. Given the importance of international monetary
               relationships, this omission is a serious shortcoming of the
               book. Alternative historical interpretations of different policy
               actions also are not explored. For example, the book's
               description of Fed policy during the 1920s follows that of Lester
               Chandler (1958). There is no mention that Milton Friedman and
               Anna J. Schwartz (1963) and Elmus Wicker (1966) have
               substantially different views. Nor is there much discussion of
               political or interest group influence on policy. But, on the
               whole, I believe the book is well done and would make useful
               reading for students in money and banking and monetary history
               courses.",
  month     =  sep,
  year      =  1988,
  language  = "en"
}

