               concentrated among a few firms relative to the indentured or free
               passenger trade. Typically, indentured servants had four-year
               contracts which sold for just enough to cover passage costs. The
               seven years of servitude required of convicts, coupled with a
               three to five pound per head government transportation subsidy,
               suggests that convict transportation was relatively profitable.
