               answered satisfactorily. Why didn't competition bid away these
               profits? How did shippers compete for government contracts and
               how would this competition influence the size of government
               subsidies and the length of convict servitude? Who ultimately
               received the profits: politicians, jailers, shippers, or
               planters? Finally, why were most convicts shipped to the
               Chesapeake? Shippers were free to take them to any colony. The
               availability of tobacco as return freight does not fully explain
               the pattern because profitable freight was available in other
               colonies. While the answers appear to be within reach, the book
               does not provide them. The most interesting aspect of Ekirch's
               study of convicts in colonial society is their contribution to
               crime. The fear that convict transportation would cause a
               colonial crime wave appears misplaced. Ekirch shows that convicts
               committed proportionally fewer property crimes than other
