               obtain unbiased estimates of the treatment effect without
               conditioning on a possibly high-dimensional vector of
               pre-treatment variables. Although adjusting for the propensity
               score removes all the bias, this can come at the expense of
               efficiency. We show that weighting with the inverse of a
               nonparametric estimate of the propensity score, rather than the
