  number    =  3,
  pages     = "545--561",
  abstract  = "AbstractWe study the effects of audits on long run compliance
               behavior using a random audit program covering more than 53,000
               tax returns. We find that audits raise reported tax liabilities
               for five years after audit, effects are longer-lasting for more
               stable sources of income, and only individuals found to have made
               errors respond to audit. A total of 60\%–65\% of revenue from
               audit comes from the change in reporting behavior. Extending the
               standard model of rational tax evasion, we show that these
               results are best explained by information revealed by audits
